persona_gamma:
The quiet onset of this trouble became clear yesterday afternoon. My son was playing in the dark space beneath the washbasin. I keep containers of substance there to keep the small crawling creatures away, though I remained convinced he had not touched these things. My eldest daughter, who watches him with a vigilant eye, observed him near the basins and warned me that he must not be left there, which prompted me to move him immediately. However, when the sun rose today, the boy's belly began to turn against itself in distress, and he suffered a flow of dark drops from his nose that would not cease. I felt it my solemn duty to bring him here, as his father has been waiting at home to hear that our child is restored to balance.

We obtained immediate laboratory studies: 
WBC: 6,000/microliter; 
Hgb: 11.2 g/dL; 
Platelets: 200,000/microliter; 
PTT: 35 seconds; 
INR: 6.5; 
Na: 140 mEq/L; 
K: 4 mEq/L; 
Cr: 0.7 mg/dL. 

There is no shadow of sickness in his breath, his heart remains steady, and his muscles hold no complaint. Which of the following is likely to be the most appropriate treatment?
